{"content":{"activity_type":"event_msg/item_completed","completed_at_ms":1790304005542,"item":{"aggregated_output":"    94\t`{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` in `~/.turnbridge/config.json`.\n    95\t\n    96\tSee [product intent](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md), the\n    97\t[roadmap](docs/ROADMAP.md), and the\n    98\t[WIP technical design](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md).\n    99\t\n   100\t## Development\n   101\t\n   102\t```sh\n   103\tnpm install   # links ../conversation-ledger\n   104\tnpm test      # build + node --test\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\t## License\n   108\t\n   109\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n   288\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n   289\t- Cost of enabling: like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for remembered values. Re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage. The store is not reversible, but exact lengths, four bucket bits, and digests make it a guess-checking oracle: low-entropy human passwords remain dictionary-attackable. Per-store salting prevents reusable cross-store/rainbow-table work; generated high-entropy tokens remain the intended case.\n   290\t\n   291\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push, but the default output contains only aggregate counts and safe next-step guidance — no fingerprints or event coordinates. A human can deliberately request the coordinate-only report with `cledger records sync --report` (or `cledger scan --report` for the standalone scan). That report prints event id, rule, JSON path + offset, and fingerprint, but never a character of the flagged text or its surroundings. It is **grouped by fingerprint**: the same span recurring across an event's `content` and `raw` mirrors, or across every edit of one file, is one decision, and the report's size tracks decisions rather than match sites (this repo's own dogfood backlog was 153 sites that collapse to 11 spans). Coding agents should not request the report: even contentless credential-shaped coordinates can feed back into captured work. Readable content lives behind `cledger records review` (interactive, one screen per span) and `cledger records inspect --output FILE` (writes a `0600` file); both refuse to run inside a coding-agent session. The pre-push hook always uses concise output; a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n   292\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n   293\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n   294\t- Remediation paths: `cledger records review` (walk every outstanding span), `cledger records redact EVENT_ID --pattern REGEX` (real secrets), `cledger records allow FINGERPRINT` (false positives), `cledger records sync --no-scan` (human-only push bypass). `--paranoid` adds entropy-based detection to sync; review and inspect use `--tier paranoid`.\n   295\t- Scan-tier heuristics skip a match whose span carries an uppercase fixture marker (`FAKE`, `EXAMPLE`, `PLACEHOLDER`, `DUMMY`, `NOTREAL`, `TESTONLY`) — see \"Writing secret-shaped fixtures\" below. Capture-tier rules never honor the marker: they match real token formats, and a real leaked token could contain those bytes by chance.\n   296\t\n   297\t**Allowlist tiers**: a false positive is usually a property of the *text* (a fixture, a doc example), not of the repo it was captured in — the same span gets re-flagged in the next repo that quotes the same doc. `cledger records allow FINGERPRINT` records it for this repo (`.git/conversation-ledger/allowlist.json`); add `--global` for every repo on this machine (`~/.config/cledger/allowlist.json`); and a repo can commit shared entries in `.cledger.json` as `{\"scan\": {\"allowFingerprints\": [\"...\"]}}` so clones and teammates inherit them. All three union at scan time. Fingerprints are truncated `sha256` of a span a human judged *not* to be a secret, so committing them discloses nothing.\n   298\t\n   299\t### Commands\n   300\t\n   301\tThe canonical maintenance interface is `cledger records <command>`. These\n   302\tshortcuts use the same dispatcher: `cledger sync`, `review`, `inspect`,\n   303\t`redact`, `allow`, and `re-anchor`. The installed hook continues to call\n   304\ttop-level `cledger transport-push`; `cledger records transport-push` is also\n   305\tavailable. `cledger scan` remains cledger's standalone check.\n   306\t\n   307\t| Command after `cledger records` | Purpose |\n   308\t| --- | --- |\n   309\t| `sync [remote] [--fetch-only\\|--push-only] [--all] [--no-scan] [--paranoid] [--report]` | Fetch, merge, or push the notes ref. Default scope is HEAD; `--all` shares the whole ledger. `--no-scan` can push only from a human session. |\n   310\t| `review [--tier standard\\|paranoid] [--context N]` | Interactive finding review. Human only; requires TTY stdin and stdout. |\n   311\t| `inspect --output FILE [--tier standard\\|paranoid] [--context N] [--reveal]` | Write finding context to a private file. Human only; `--overwrite` permits replacing a file. |\n   312\t| `redact EVENT_ID (--pattern REGEX\\|--all) [--reason TEXT]` | Rewrite an unshared event and append an audit event. Human only. |\n   313\t| `allow FINGERPRINT... [--global]` | Accept false positives locally or globally. Human only. |\n   314\t| `reanchor [--target REV] [--apply]` | Report rewrite mappings; optionally apply exact matches. |\n   315\t| `reanchor manual OLD_REV... --onto NEW_REV` | Assert a mapping. Human only. |\n   316\t\n   317\t`cledger records --help` prints the full command syntax. Exit status is `0`\n   318\tfor success, `1` for findings, policy refusals, partial results or runtime\n   319\terrors, and `2` for invalid usage. `sync --no-scan` is human only when it can\n   320\tpush; fetch-only use has no sharing gate. Review, inspect, redact, allow, and\n   321\tmanual reanchoring refuse coding-agent sessions. The pre-push hook lets the\n   322\tcode push continue after ordinary ledger errors; only a scan block with\n   323\t`transport.strict` enabled aborts the code push.\n   324\t\n   325\tThe standalone annals CLI can use this exact namespace through a profile:\n   326\t\n   327\t```sh\n   328\tannals profile add conversation-ledger \\\n   329\t  --namespace conversation-ledger \\\n   330\t  --incoming cledger-incoming \\\n   331\t  --internal-env CLEDGER_INTERNAL \\\n   332\t  --state-dir conversation-ledger \\\n   333\t  --config-file .cledger.json \\\n   334\t  --user-config-dir cledger \\\n   335\t  --cli-name cledger\n   336\tannals --profile conversation-ledger sync\n   337\t```\n   338\t\n   339\tThe profile does not install cledger's hook; that executable path stays with\n   340\tcledger itself.\n   341\t\n   342\t`cledger records redact EVENT_ID (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`\n   343\trewrites a stored event to a placeholder and audit metadata, appends a\n   560\t  [\"inspect\", \"inspect\"],\n   561\t  [\"redact\", \"redact\"],\n   562\t  [\"allow\", \"allow\"],\n   563\t  [\"re-anchor\", \"reanchor\"],\n   564\t]);\n   565\t\n   566\tasync function cmdRecords(argv: string[]): Promise<void> {\n   567\t  const repo = await findRepo(process.cwd());\n   568\t  if (!repo) {\n   569\t    process.stderr.write(\"cledger: not inside a git repository\\n\");\n   570\t    process.exitCode = 2;\n   571\t    return;\n   572\t  }\n   573\t  process.exitCode = await runRecordsCommand({\n   574\t    ledger: asLedger(repo),\n   575\t    argv,\n   576\t    stdin: process.stdin,\n   577\t    stdout: process.stdout,\n   578\t    stderr: process.stderr,\n   579\t    env: process.env,\n   580\t    commandName: \"cledger records\",\n   581\t  });\n   582\t}\n   583\t\n   584\tasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n   585\t  guardStdout();\n   586\t  const [, , command, ...rest] = process.argv;\n   587\t\n   588\t  if (!command || command === \"--help\" || command === \"help\") {\n   589\t    process.stdout.write(USAGE + \"\\n\");\n   590\t    return;\n   591\t  }\n   592\t  if (command === \"--version\") {\n   593\t    process.stdout.write(version() + \"\\n\");\n   594\t    return;\n   595\t  }\n   596\t  if (command === \"records\") return cmdRecords(rest);\n   597\t  if (command === \"transport-push\") return cmdTransportPush(rest[0]);\n   598\t  const alias = RECORD_ALIASES.get(command);\n   599\t  if (alias) return cmdRecords([alias, ...rest]);\n   600\t  const { positional, flags } = parseArgs(rest);\n   601\t  switch (command) {\n   602\t    case \"append\":\n   603\t      return cmdAppend(flags);\n   604\t    case \"log\":\n   605\t      return cmdLog(flags);\n   606\t    case \"show\":\n   607\t      return cmdShow(positional, flags);\n   608\t    case \"conversations\":\n   609\t      return cmdConversations(flags);\n   610\t    case \"export\":\n   611\t      return cmdExport(flags);\n   612\t    case \"scan\":\n   613\t      return cmdScan(flags);\n   614\t    case \"renormalize\":\n   615\t      return cmdRenormalize();\n","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","nl -ba README.md | sed -n '94,110p'; nl -ba ../conversation-ledger/README.md | sed -n '288,343p'; nl -ba ../conversation-ledger/src/cli.ts | sed -n '560,615p'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","duration":{"nanos":7042,"secs":0},"exit_code":0,"formatted_output":"    94\t`{\"reasoningRepla