{"content":{"activity_type":"event_msg/task_complete","completed_at":1790978563,"duration_ms":81795,"last_agent_message":"I reviewed the recent cledger PRs. [PR #26](https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger/pull/26) is the relevant model: 20 CLI capture integrations, pinned installed binaries, real TUI drivers, isolated profiles, scripted local providers, and separate live-model evidence. It remains open; its description notes another hosted rerun pending.\n\nTurnbridge currently supports three targets—Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode. Its [interactive smoke test](/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/scripts/smoke-interactive.mjs) already launches real TUIs, but checks imported markers in terminal output without submitting a new prompt. It also writes into normal session storage. The next step should prove the whole bridge: history renders, history reaches model context, continuation works, and cledger captures the continuation with correct lineage.\n\nBefore implementation, I’d like your answers to these:\n\n1. **CLI scope:** Should we aim for the same 20-CLI roster as cledger, or prioritize a first batch? Gemini and Qwen are already on Turnbridge’s roadmap.\n2. **What counts as support?** Is a clearly labeled fresh session bootstrapped from a transcript acceptable where native history import is unavailable, or must every supported target show the imported conversation in its native scrollback?\n3. **Acceptance bar:** Should each target require rendered history, model-context verification, a successful new tool call, automatic continuation capture, and a subsequent native resume? I recommend all five.\n4. **Platforms:** Should installed TUI tests on both macOS and Linux be required from the first release?\n5. **Inference tiers:** Should we follow cledger’s split—free scripted-provider CI, local DeepSeek canaries where compatible, and separately authorized paid-provider checks? How should account-gated CLIs qualify for release?\n6. **Maintenance:** Do you want review-first scheduled pin-update PRs like cledger, or initially just verification and drift reports?\n\nThe main design decisions we’ll need to settle are:\n\n- **Capabilities per CLI.** Source capture, native resume, native import, and transcript bootstrap are different capabilities. The picker should disclose the available mode rather than give every CLI one “supported” flag.\n- **Reuse across repositories.** I’d reuse cledger’s runtime provisioning, PTY handling, provider fixtures, and evidence conventions through a deliberate shared interface. Turnbridge should own the bridge-specific assertions.\n- **What the tests actually prove.** Terminal output containing a marker doesn’t establish correct screen layout. We should inspect terminal screen state and separately inspect outbound model context; live-model recall adds another layer of evidence.\n- **Matrix size.** Twenty CLIs create 380 directed cross-CLI combinations. I’d start with canonical-history tests for every target, capture compatibility for every source, and selected real round trips and multi-hop chains.\n- **History fidelity.** We need explicit behavior for attachments, tool results, visible thinking, unknown records, and long histories—especially where a target cannot represent them natively.\n- **Isolation and version policy.** Tests should use disposable profiles and session stores. Exact tested versions should be recorded, with an explicit policy for fallback when an installed version is unverified.\n\nNo files changed. Implementation can wait until cledger #26 is finished and merged, then use its final verification infrastructure as the baseline.","started_at":1790978481,"time_to_first_token_ms":2382,"turn_id":"01a0fea2-de7f-7560-aedd-a33eb96689ce"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"0008bdd9e01153e600aff87b81fb64457d9a0073","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"id":"ev2-5308e335cfadf786688a0c75792cc6cf9844bf1